#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Run the C CLI and the Python one side by side and compare what they do.

Development only; nothing in the plugin runs this. The Python CLI is the
reference: it is taken with `git archive` from REFERENCE, the last commit that
shipped it, so the tree being tested never needs to contain it -- and it keeps
working after the Python CLI is gone from main.

Each case starts from a home seeded by the reference, copied twice, so both
sides begin from byte-identical state. Every external program either CLI can
run -- the notifier, the shell, hyprctl, the picker, OCR, ffmpeg, the agents --
is a stub that records how it was called, so a case never touches the real
desktop and the calls themselves are compared.

Compared: exit code; stdout, index.json and config.json parsed as JSON (so
spacing and key order are not differences); the database, schema and every
row; the log; the files left in the data and state directories; and every
recorded call, including the prompt an agent was sent. stderr is not compared:
the wording of errors is allowed to change.

Where the C side differs on purpose, the case says so in EXPECTED, with a
normaliser that has to make the two agree -- so a declared difference is still
a checked one.

    dev/parity                 all cases
    dev/parity commit          cases whose name contains "commit"
    dev/parity --ref <commit>  a different reference
    dev/parity --cc gcc        a different compiler
    dev/parity --sanitize      the C side under ASan and UBSan
    dev/parity --dump item     print what the C side did, to check a case tests something
"""

